the supreme court plans to review  affirmative action policies in cases  against harvard and the university of  north carolina this consideration of  race and school admissions has long been  a polarizing topic and has come up  several times in recent decades  any shift when the justices hear the  cases later this year would set a new  legal precedent so i want to bring in  ucla school of law assistant professor  latoya baldwin clark now to get some  clarity professor thank you so much for  joining us welcome to quick take there's  obviously a lot to unpack here starting  with the fact that we have a supreme  court in which six of the nine justices  are conservatives what does it signal  that the court has agreed to hear these  cases involving harvard and unc  considering race in their admissions  process  yeah so you know  what the court is signaling i think is  that um after the last affirmative  action cases um the court was very clear  that using race as part of a holistic  idea of how students should be  gathered as a class for diversity sake  um that was just a few years ago that  was just in 2016.  and therefore the fact that they're  taking up this case again this type of  case again in such a short amount of  time i think really just signals the  changing  political persuasion of the court rather  than any new evidence that has come to  light about affirmative action  interesting you mentioned the 2016 case  i want to go back to 2003 because the  decision known as gruder versus  bollinger reaffirmed that universities  can take race into account so i wonder  um how does it lay the groundwork for  how the supreme court justices might  think about the upcoming cases that  they're considering  sure so the groundwork in that case said  that universities even public  universities are able to use race as a  factor and considering putting together  an entire class for the sake of  diversity and quite honestly um gretter  is a direct precedent for the case that  is coming up right now i mean so it's  going to be difficult i think for the  plaintiffs to actually make a case as to  why we should change now and that  precedent that has been sitting like you  said for you know over 10 years um it's  not clear to me exactly how the um how  the plaintiffs in this case are actually  going to  unprevail given uh the decision  ingredient okay well what i found  interesting in that greta case is that  the majority opinion from sandra day  o'connor uh was that racial preferences  would no longer be necessary in 25 years  25 years has come uh and gone  well it hasn't yet but um it's  interesting how she's framed things in a  certain way and that hasn't exactly come  to pass how do you think that will  influence their thinking  i mean that's a great question i think  that when justice o'connor put that in  her opinion many people were very  surprised as it wasn't necessary to  actually settle that case now of course  i think the plaintiffs are going to use  those words when they come before the  supreme court again and the supreme  court might ask themselves how different  is it now today than it was almost 25  years ago and my answer would be it's  actually not very different now at all i  don't think that we have a new set of  facts that would allow us to overturn  the precedent that was settled in greta  well a different group of people might  argue otherwise um when i applied to  college for instance being asian helped  me or at least didn't hurt me all things  being equal when my kids apply to  college they will be  an overrepresented minority as asians so  if you could talk a little bit about how  the relationship of race and education  educational access specific specifically  have evolved or have changed over the  last 18 years since grunder  yeah i mean i think that they've changed  in ways this this case is very  interesting because it does focus on  asian americans where generally when in  the past we've been thinking about  affirmative action from this very black  white um binary um but i think that what  has happened is that asian americans are  typically over represented  in these universities and that's not to  say that asian americans don't  necessarily deserve to be there it's  more of the factual point and that we  have an over-representation of  asian-americans  in schools and there's been some recent  data and some recent studies that says  even if we were to get rid of  affirmative action and only look at  things for example test scores the  actual percentage of asian americans  that would be admitted into these elite  universities would not actually change  all that much yeah there was a study  from georgetown that showed that the  percentage of asian americans would go  down by one percentage points and  actually the biggest beneficiaries would  be uh wealthy white men interestingly  enough in if they were to only look at  standardized test scores i know that  your specialty professor baldwin clark  is examining race and education at the  k-12 level and there's been a lot of  concerns or a lot of talk about  whether race should be a factor as well  in uh testing into selective high  schools i'm curious to hear what you  think are the common threads the  overlaps between race and education at  the k-12 level and race and education at  the university level are these good  comparisons to make  um i don't think that there are good  comparisons to make except for the fact  of when we're talking about elite  universities and elite schools uh k-12  schools i would actually think that we  need to look beyond just thinking about  the elite schools both at the high  school level and  at the university level because um black  children brown children and indigenous  children are suffering in their own  schools that are not necessarily elite  universities um the elite universities  or elite high schools um so i would  argue that we need to look broader than  that elite universities only make up a  very small amount of the colleges and  universities in our country and in those  colleges and universities affirmative  action is actually not a very big deal  what we should be trying to do  especially at the k-12 level is making  sure that all of our children have an  opportunity to be able to go to elite  university if they choose to and not  only through the elective the um elite  and selective high schools yeah legacy  and athletics actually probably play a  more prominent role than affirmative  action if anything one word that you  mentioned and that comes up a lot when  talking about college admissions is  holistic colleges defend their holistic  admissions process which is pretty much  a squishy term because it can mean  whatever the school wants it to mean in  whatever circumstance or context that  they happen to be in would abolishing  the consideration of race mean that  there is still room to consider other  factors like socio-economic background  or geography  that may intersect with race  yes absolutely and i think that's what a  lot of the um proponents of winning this  case would say is that not looking at  race well why don't we look at social  economics why don't we look at geography  why don't we look at all these other  things that actually may still bring the  diversity that we care about but through  these indirect uh mechanisms  yeah and i i also love what you said  about how this is a very specific issue  to elite high schools and elite colleges  and the world is a lot bigger than elite  schools  what is interesting to me as well in  these upcoming cases is the government's  stance because the trump  administration's department of justice  supported the lawsuits but justice under  the biden administration  supports the defendant here which is  harvard how big a role do you think the  doj will play  in the case can they affect the outcome  or maybe that's a wrong question to ask  can they affect the arguments that are  being put forth 